{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. His minority led to a regency led by his mother and later by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond was known for his cautious approach to warfare and diplomacy. He spent much of his early rule consolidating power within his own territory and avoiding direct conflict with Muslim neighbors. His reign began during a time of growing tension between the Crusader states and the rising power of Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the internal politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a rival of the powerful House of Ibelin and often clashed with other nobles over control of the throne. In 1174 he became regent of Jerusalem after the death of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was chosen as regent because of his experience and noble lineage. His regency was marked by indecision and internal divisions among the nobility. He failed to unite the barons and was criticized for his reluctance to confront Saladin. His leadership during this period was seen as weak and contributed to the decline of Crusader military strength. He was eventually replaced as regent by Guy of Lusignan who was married to Baldwin IVs sister Sibylla.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was involved in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187. He had warned against the rash decision to march to Tiberias and had advocated for a defensive strategy. However his advice was ignored and he was forced to join the campaign. During the battle he was captured by Saladin and spent time in captivity. After 